# We use `.env` files to store environment variables for our project. We load
# environment variables from the following files:
#
# 1. `.env.$(NODE_ENV).local` (ignored by `.gitignore`)
# 2. `.env.$(NODE_ENV)`
# 3. `.env`
#
# In production, environment variables are securely configured in the service
# running our code. For instance Cloudflare Workers has their own environment
# variable configuration UI.
#
# Avaliable environment variables:
#
# - `APP_DEV_PORT`: Port for the main application server.
#
# - `APP_DEV_PRIVATE_PORT`: In development this is the port our application
#   server actually runs on. Then we run a proxy on `APP_DEV_PORT`.
#
# - `BAZEL_DEV_SERVER_PORT`: Port for Remix's WebSocket development server.
#   Manages live reload among other things.
#
# - `DYNAMO_LOCAL_PORT`: Port where we can access our local DynamoDB database.
#   Uses: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DynamoDBLocal.html
#
# - `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` and `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`: AWS access key variables.
#   If these are not set then our code will use local mocks of AWS services.
#
# - `AWS_BEDROCK_TOKEN`: Optional Bedrock auth token used by experimental media
#   tagging scripts and other direct Bedrock experiments in development.
#
# - `HONEYCOMB_API_KEY`: The API key to use when communicating with Honeycomb.
#   Each developer gets their own development environment and API key. This is
#   private to each developer and should go in your `.env.development.local`
#   file.
#
# - `EDGE_DEV_PORT`: Port for edge service in development. In production our
#   edge service runs through Cloudflare Workers directly on our domain
#   https://alpine.inc.
#
# - `EDGE_DEV_PRIVATE_PORT`: In development this is the port edge service
#   actually runs on. Then we run a proxy on `EDGE_DEV_PORT`.
#
# - `OPENSEARCH_LOCAL_PORT`: Port where we can access our local OpenSearch
#   database. Runs the Java executable on our local machine.
#
# - `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY`: The Stripe API key for handling payments.
#   This should be in each developer's `.env.development.local`
#   file when working on billing-related features.
#
# - `STRIPE_SIGNING_SECRET`: The Stripe signing secret for verifying webhook
#   requests. This should be in each developer's `.env.development.local`
#   file when working on billing-related features. See server/billing/README.md
#   for more information.
#
# - `SLACK_CLIENT_ID`: The Slack client ID for the Alpine app, used to uniquely
#    identify our app to the Slack API.
#
# - `SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET`: The Slack client secret for the Alpine app, used to
#    authenticate our app to the Slack API.
#
# - `SLACK_AUTH_REDIRECT_ORIGIN`: The origin of the Slack OAuth redirect that Slack uses to
#    redirect back to our app once the user has authorized the app. This *must* use HTTPS and
#    be registered in the Slack app settings.
#
# - `SEND_ALERT_ALERTS_CHANNEL_ID`, `SEND_ALERT_BETA_ALERTS_CHANNEL_ID`,
#   `SEND_ALERT_HONEYCOMB_CHANNEL_ID`, `SEND_ALERT_BUILDS_CHANNEL_ID`, and
#   `SEND_ALERT_GITHUB_CHANNEL_ID`: Optional channel IDs used by the `send_alert`
#   Lambda. If unset, the Lambda defaults to the current production alert
#   channels. Set these in `.env.development.local` when simulating webhooks
#   against local channels.
